.TH STPCPY 3 2026-08-17 "Substrate LibC" "Substrate Library Functions"
.SH NAME
stpcpy \- copy a string, returning a pointer to its end
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RI ( libc ,
.BR \-lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <string.h>
.sp
.BI "char *stpcpy(char *" dest ", const char *" src ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR stpcpy ()
function copies the string pointed to by
.I src
(including the terminating null byte ('\\0')) to the buffer pointed to by
.IR dest .
It behaves exactly as
.BR strcpy (3)
except for what it returns.
.sp
The strings may not overlap, and
.I dest
must be large enough to receive the copy.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR stpcpy ()
returns a pointer to the terminating null byte it wrote in
.IR dest ,
rather than to the start of
.IR dest .
This is what makes it useful: a caller appending several pieces can carry the
returned pointer forward instead of rescanning what it just wrote, turning a
sequence of
.BR strcat (3)
calls from quadratic into linear.
.SH ERRORS
No errors are defined.
.BR stpcpy ()
does not set
.IR errno .
.SH EXAMPLE
Concatenate three strings with a single pass over the output:
.sp
.nf
.RS
char buf[64];
char *p = buf;

p = stpcpy(p, "substrate");
p = stpcpy(p, "/");
p = stpcpy(p, "libc");
/* buf now holds "substrate/libc", p points at its NUL */
.RE
.fi
.SH CONFORMING TO
POSIX.1-2008.  Also present in glibc and the BSDs.
.SH BUGS
.BR stpcpy ()
is exactly as unbounded as
.BR strcpy (3):
it has no idea how large
.I dest
is and will happily run off the end of it.  Prefer
.BR strlcpy (3),
which takes the destination size, unless the returned end pointer is
specifically what you need.
.sp
The companion function
.BR stpncpy (3)
is not implemented.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR strcpy (3),
.BR strlcpy (3),
.BR strcat (3),
.BR strlcat (3),
.BR memcpy (3)
